NAME
    basic - the BASIC interpreter

SYNOPSIS
    basic
    basic file
    basic -r file

DESCRIPTION
    Y1 BASIC: 16-bit integers, strings, and programs kept in files.
    basic alone shows the prompt "> ". basic file LOADs the file
    first; basic -r file LOADs it, RUNs it and returns to the shell,
    so a BASIC program can be a command or a line of a script. BYE,
    or Ctrl-D at an empty prompt, returns to the shell.

    A line that starts with a number is stored in the program (the
    same number again replaces the line, the number alone deletes it);
    a line without one runs at once. Keywords may be typed in any
    case; LIST shows them in capitals. Several statements go on one
    line separated by ":".

    Statements: PRINT (";" joins, "," puts a space, a trailing ; or ,
    keeps the line open), LET (optional), IF expr THEN statement or
    line number, FOR v = a TO b [STEP s] ... NEXT [v], GOTO n,
    GOSUB n ... RETURN, INPUT ["prompt";] v or v$, POKE addr, byte,
    REM, END.
    Commands: RUN, LIST, NEW, SAVE "file", LOAD "file", HELP, BYE.
    Files: OPEN "file" FOR OUTPUT or FOR INPUT, PRINT# value, INPUT#
    v or v$ (one value per line), EOF(1) (1 at the end of the file),
    CLOSE. One file is open at a time; RUN and the end of a program
    close it.
    Functions: ABS(x) RND(n) (1..n) PEEK(addr) LEN(s$) ASC(s$)
    VAL(s$) CHR$(n) LEFT$(s$,n) RIGHT$(s$,n) MID$(s$,start[,n])
    STR$(n).
    Operators: + - * / % (remainder), = <> < > <= >= (1 true, 0
    false; numbers compare signed, strings by character code), and
    + joins strings. Numbers are written in decimal or as 0x1F.

    SAVE writes the program as text, exactly as LIST shows it, so a
    saved program can be read with cat, changed with vi and LOADed
    back. LOAD replaces the program in memory with the file's lines.

    Ctrl-C stops a running program with "BREAK IN n". Any other key
    typed while a program runs is kept for its next INPUT or the next
    prompt, but a key typed while basic -r runs is lost when the
    program ends.

ERRORS
    ?SYNTAX ERROR [IN n]   the line cannot be read; when typed, a
                           line with unbalanced () or "" or one that
                           starts with THEN, TO, a function... is
                           refused and not stored
    ?UNDEF'D LINE n IN m   GOTO or GOSUB to a line that is not there
    ?ONLY IN A PROGRAM     GOTO, GOSUB, RETURN or NEXT typed at the
                           prompt
    ?RETURN WITHOUT GOSUB, ?NEXT WITHOUT FOR, ?GOSUB TOO DEEP,
    ?FOR TOO DEEP, ?TOO MANY VARIABLES, ?TOO MANY STRINGS,
    ?OUT OF MEMORY, ?NO FILE, ?CANNOT SAVE, ?CANNOT OPEN

LIMITS
    Numbers -32768..32767 (whole numbers only; / rounds toward 0, a
    division by 0 gives 0). Names: 6 significant letters or digits,
    32 number variables and 16 strings of up to 32 characters. FOR and
    GOSUB nest 8 deep. Program lines up to 98 characters; 12286 bytes
    of program. No arrays.

EXAMPLES
    > 10 for i = 1 to 3 : print i, i*i : next i
    > run
    > save "squares.bas"
    > bye
    /> basic -r squares.bas

NOTES
    Ported from P8X basic/basic.c 2026-11-09, without its graphics
    statements. New: programs saved as text, basic -r, INPUT with a
    prompt, Ctrl-C, deeper FOR and GOSUB.
    The ROM has its own, smaller BASIC (the monitor's I command); it
    cannot load or save programs.

SEE ALSO
    vi, cat, sh
